UNIT parse_type
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@bb8ded84fa50 cli/sir/canon.mjs parseType — the strict abstract type grammar every SIG type is marshalled
  by; reproduce the code exactly
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG parse_type(s: text) -> json
ENVELOPE s is well-formed text (no lone surrogate), of any length and any nesting. Throws are IN scope as observations
  (R11). Every result R1–R10 can yield is carriable by the value codec (R10), so the depth boundary itself — MAX_DEPTH
  parses, MAX_DEPTH + 1 throws — is in the oracle for every composite form.
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION Parse the text of one abstract SIG type into a type node: a map whose member k names the form, plus the
  members TABLE NODE lists for that form. Forms: the eight scalar names, tuple[…], list[…], map[…], record[name: T, …],
  and unions written with "|". The whole text must be exactly one type, and no union alternative may be unreachable (an
  earlier alternative covers it: R6); anything else throws.
BEHAVIOR
R1 WHITESPACE is the WS set only. Any run of it may stand before or after any token — between a composite's name and its
  "[" too ("list [text]" and "list\n[text]" parse) — and at either end of the text, and is ignored there. It may not
  stand inside a name. Every other character outside a name or a token — a no-break space, a form feed, a vertical tab,
  any non-ASCII character — is not whitespace and makes the text throw.
R2 The text is one TYPE (R3) and nothing after it but whitespace; leftover text throws ("text none", "list[text] x").
R3 TYPE = an ALT, then zero or more of: "|" followed by an ALT. Two or more alts make a union whose alts are the nodes in
  written order, nothing reordered. A single alt is its own node. Every "|" must be followed by an alt ("text |",
  "text || none" and "| text" throw). An alt is never itself a union node: a union inside a union exists only as a part of
  a composite.
R4 UNREACHABLE ALTERNATIVE: a union throws when some alt is COVERED (R6) by an alt written EARLIER in the same union,
  adjacent or not. Order matters: "text | json" and "none | json" parse; "json | text", "json | none" and "json | json"
  throw. The rule holds at every level ("list[i64 | i64]" and "list[json | f64]" throw), and is checked once all of that
  union's alts are read.
R5 SAME TYPE: two nodes are the same type when their k is equal and — tuple: the same count of items, pairwise the same
  type in written order; list: the items the same type; map: keys the same type and vals the same type; record: the same
  set of field names, each name's two types the same, field ORDER ignored; union: the same set of alts up to the same
  type, alt ORDER ignored; a scalar: nothing more. Whitespace never matters. So "list[text | none] | list[none | text]"
  and "record[a: text, b: i64] | record[b: i64, a: text]" throw, while "tuple[text, i64] | tuple[i64, text]",
  "record[a: text] | record[b: text]" and "record[a: text, b: i64] | record[b: text, a: i64]" parse.
R6 COVERS: an earlier alt E covers a later alt L exactly when one of these holds, applied recursively to the parts:
  (a) E and L are the same type (R5);
  (b) E is json and L is JSON-SHAPED: none, bool, f64, text or json; a list whose item is JSON-SHAPED; a tuple every item of
  which is; a map whose key is exactly the scalar text and whose val is; a record every field type of which is; a union
  every alt of which is;
  (c) both are lists and E's item covers L's item;
  (d) both are tuples of the same item count and each item of E covers L's item at the same position;
  (e) both are maps, E's key covers L's key and E's val covers L's val;
  (f) both are records and every field of E has a field of the same name in L that E's field type covers — L may have MORE
  fields than E, never fewer ("record[a: text] | record[a: text, b: none]" throws, the reverse order parses);
  (g) E is a union and some alt of E covers L;
  (h) L is a union and E covers every alt of L.
  (g) and (h) arise only on parts, since an alt is never a union. Nothing else covers: bytes, i64 and u64 are never
  JSON-SHAPED; a map keyed by anything but the bare scalar text is not ("json | map[text | none, json]" parses); no form
  covers another form ("list[text] | tuple[text]", "map[text, text] | record[a: text]" and "f64 | i64" parse); and coverage
  is by ONE earlier alt, never by several jointly ("tuple[text] | tuple[none] | tuple[text | none]" parses).
R7 ALT begins with a NAME, the longest run of IDENT characters there (the IDENT pin). A NAME equal to a scalar of TABLE
  NODE -> that scalar node. A NAME equal to tuple, list, map or record must be followed (after optional whitespace) by "["
  and continues as R8 or R9. Every other NAME throws (case-sensitive: "Text", "text2", "textnone", "option" throw).
R8 COMPOSITE: inside the brackets, one or more TYPEs (R3, so a part may be a union) separated by ",", then "]". No empty
  part and no trailing comma. tuple takes any count of one or more as its items; list takes exactly one as its item; map
  takes exactly two, key then val. Any other count throws ("tuple[]", "list[text, i64]", "map[text]").
R9 RECORD: inside the brackets, one or more FIELDs separated by ",", then "]". FIELD = a name matching the IDENT pin,
  ":", a TYPE (R3). Field names are unique within one record, compared exactly (case-sensitive: "A" and "a" differ); a
  repeated name throws. A field name may equal a type name ("record[text: text]"). Fields keep written order.
R10 NESTING CAP: composites (tuple, list, map, record) nest at most MAX_DEPTH deep. The depth of a composite is the count
  of composites enclosing it plus one; siblings do not add up ("tuple[list[i64], list[i64]]" is depth 2). A text whose
  composites reach depth MAX_DEPTH parses; one that reaches MAX_DEPTH + 1 throws — however deep it goes, it throws and
  never exhausts the host. Scalars and unions add no type depth, but they do add RESULT depth: counting the root node as
  level 0 and each member value, list element and field pair one deeper, a record-of-union chain costs 5 levels per
  composite, the most of any form, so the deepest legal result is 5 × MAX_DEPTH + 3 levels — inside MAX_VALUE_DEPTH.
R11 Brackets therefore always balance: a missing "]", an extra "]" or a stray "[" leaves R2–R9 unsatisfied and throws.
  Every violation of R1–R10 throws, wherever it sits in the nesting; the message is not graded.
TABLE NODE — the members of each node (a json map; member order is immaterial, the canonical form sorts keys)
  scalar | k = one of text, bytes, bool, none, i64, u64, f64, json
  union  | k = "union"; alts = list of nodes, at least two, no alt covered by an earlier one (R4), never a union node
  tuple  | k = "tuple"; items = list of nodes, at least one
  list   | k = "list"; item = a node
  map    | k = "map"; key = a node; val = a node
  record | k = "record"; fields = list of two-element lists [name text, node], at least one, in written order
TABLE DIALS
  MAX_DEPTH       | 16
  MAX_VALUE_DEPTH | 100 (the value codec's nesting cap; above 5 × MAX_DEPTH + 3, so it never bounds the oracle)
PINNED charset WS = the code points 0009 000A 000D 0020 (hexadecimal: tab, line feed, carriage return, space) CITED R1 — the grammar header of canon.mjs; form feed, vertical tab, no-break space and FEFF are NOT members
PINNED grammar IDENT = an ASCII letter or underscore, then zero or more ASCII letters, ASCII digits or underscores CITED R7 and R9
OUT-OF-DOMAIN a non-text argument throws.
ORACLE value -> oracles/parse_type.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 6a97eae205a1b748daa77b03d4fbb6d02d881db33e95d07954f4792e875d2664
SCOPE IN: R1–R11 over well-formed text. OUT: the SIG line around the type (parseSig: names, rest parameters, the arrow);
  the encode and decode of values under a parsed type.
PROVENANCE rederive@bb8ded84fa50 cli/sir/canon.mjs, functions reader (its type path), typeKey, sameType, inJson, covers
  and parseType. Round 1 cut cold 2026-10-26 against b329a40412df; round 2 re-cut against the strict grammar; round 3
  against FINDINGS #44 (duplicate alts, whitespace before "[", the nesting cap); round 4 against FINDINGS #65 (coverage:
  same type up to union and field order, json-shaped alts) and #66 (the value-depth cap); round 5 against FINDINGS #74
  (structural coverage), #78 (MAX_DEPTH 16, MAX_VALUE_DEPTH 100) and #79 (json covers tuples and records of json parts).
